<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the hardened runtime (see hardenedRuntime in
  electron-builder.yml's mac: block). A hardened-runtime app is locked down
  by default - no JIT, no unsigned executable memory, no unvalidated
  libraries - and Electron plus this app's own features need several of
  those defaults relaxed to even start. None of this has been verified on
  an actual Mac yet; the reasoning below is Apple/Electron documentation
  plus first-principles reasoning about what this app specifically does,
  not firsthand confirmation.

  App Sandbox (com.apple.security.app-sandbox) is deliberately NOT present
  here. Blurt synthesizes keystrokes into whatever app the user is
  dictating into (push-to-talk paste, via Accessibility - see
  src/main/paste.ts and src/main/pushToTalk/) and spawns a user-installed
  Python interpreter as an arbitrary local child process (see
  src/main/backend/modelManager.ts, src/main/backend/sidecar.ts) - both are
  exactly what the App Sandbox exists to prevent, and a sandboxed process
  cannot use the Accessibility API at all regardless of TCC permission. Adding
  the sandbox key would not harden this app, it would break it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- Microphone capture - transcribing dictation is the entire app, and
       hardened runtime blocks device access by default without this. This
       is the entitlement; the OS-level consent prompt text is the separate
       NSMicrophoneUsageDescription key in electron-builder.yml's
       mac.extendInfo (Info.plist), not this file. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- Electron's renderer/main processes run on V8, which needs to
       JIT-compile JS at runtime. Electron's own docs call this out
       explicitly for Apple Silicon: without allow-jit, hardened-runtime
       builds on arm64 crash on launch, not on first script execution. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- Paired with allow-jit above: V8's JIT allocates executable memory
       pages that aren't backed by a signed file on disk, which hardened
       runtime otherwise refuses to execute. This is the standard
       Electron-on-hardened-runtime pairing, called out in Electron's own
       code-signing documentation alongside allow-jit. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation: considered, left
       OFF.

       Library validation (on by default under hardened runtime) requires
       every dylib/bundle/Mach-O the process loads to be signed with the
       same Team ID as the main executable, or be an Apple system library.
       Blurt loads native code in-process in exactly one place:
       uiohook-napi (src/main/pushToTalk/uiohookLoader.ts), a prebuilt
       native Node addon required in-process via `require()` for the
       global push-to-talk key hook. Everything else - the Python
       interpreter and litert-lm runtime (src/main/backend/modelManager.ts,
       sidecar.ts) - runs as a completely separate OS process via
       child_process.spawn, never dlopen'd into this process, so it is
       never subject to THIS process's library validation at all.

       For the one real in-process case (uiohook-napi), electron-builder's
       signing step (@electron/osx-sign) re-signs every Mach-O binary
       bundled inside the .app - including native node_modules addons -
       under the SAME Developer ID identity used for the main executable.
       Once re-signed that way, its Team ID matches the app's own, which is
       exactly what library validation checks for, so it should pass
       without needing this entitlement. That conclusion depends on a real
       Developer ID signing identity being used (see build:mac in
       package.json), not ad-hoc/no signing - an unsigned or ad-hoc-signed
       build is a different situation electron-builder's own docs flag
       separately (ad-hoc signing plus hardenedRuntime: true needs either
       this entitlement or hardenedRuntime: false).

       If a real macOS build turns up a library-validation rejection
       anyway, this is the first thing to flip on:
       <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
       <true/>
  -->
</dict>
</plist>
